      "explained": {
        "before": "Reranker startup had no stdin error listener, and close ended input and signalled the child without first recording a closed failure or rejecting pending requests. The cache stored worker adapters alone, while a separate set tracked their lifetimes. Worker setup captured one encoder client and one reranker process in its adapters. Ending their lifetime removed the cache entry, but already-returned adapters still held those objects. Explicit shutdown ended all lifetimes tracked in a separate set.",
        "after": "Input errors enter the terminal failure path. Close records a failure before ending input and rejects registered pending requests; the stored failure is then available to the score guard and holder replacement. Readiness and queued-call cases still need execution evidence. Each cache entry also stores its process holder. Setup and shutdown can use that same holder even when its current processes have closed; no proof run is recorded for the cache contract. Setup retains adapters around a holder with factories for both process types. Calls can resolve replacement processes through that holder,…",
        "terms": [
          {
            "term": "stdin",
            "meaning": "The pipe used to send requests to the model child process."
          },
          {
            "term": "#failure",
            "meaning": "The stored error that marks a reranker unusable."
          },
          {
            "term": "held",
            "meaning": "The map of worker adapters and their process holder, indexed by setup."
          }
        ]
      },
